WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.220
- [Narrator] Every two minutes,
there's a water main break

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.280
somewhere in the country's
more than 2.2 million miles

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.410
of pipes according to the American Society

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.760
of Civil Engineers.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.930
It's just one symptom

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.480
of the country's crumbling
water infrastructure.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.210
- Lead in the water
supply in Flint, Michigan.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.770
- The water crisis in Jackson.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.390
- Tonight, America's fourth largest city

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.280
is under a boil water order.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.990
- [Narrator] But it's not
just the leaking pipes

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.940
and contaminated drinking water.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.920
Dams and Levees that protect communities

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.170
from severe floods are also at risk.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.150
So what are some of the structural factors

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.680
driving these problems?

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.050
Here's a look at how
decades of underinvestment

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.270
brought America's water infrastructure

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.293
to a tipping point.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.940
Typically, bridges, roads, and subways

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.780
are funded by federal taxes,
but not water infrastructure.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.270
Instead, local utilities are responsible

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.450
for the operation and upkeep
of community water systems.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:55.080
This financing structure can
be traced back to the 1800s

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.590
when population growth and
industrialization led U.S. cities

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.110
and towns to rapidly
expand their water systems

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.460
for the first time.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.650
Infrastructure was
handled at a local level,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.120
not a federal one.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.820
This led to the sprawling
and highly fragmented nature

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.470
of the country's water systems.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:15.470
- There are over 50,000
utilities that manage just water.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.190
- [Narrator] Upmanu Lall is a director

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.410
at Columbia University's Water Center.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:20.700
He has led numerous studies

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.410
examining the nation's drinking water

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.970
and wastewater systems.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.950
- We either have really big utilities

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.170
or thousands of small utilities.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.230
The problem is that the
thousands of small utilities

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.990
really don't have the
technical or financial capacity

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.820
to manage things very well.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.680
- [Narrator] Today, more than 90%

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.300
of the average utilities revenues

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.130
come directly from local water bills

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.930
according to state water directors.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.450
But there's a limit to how
much money can be raised

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.610
through bills to make essential upgrades.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:48.780
Rate hikes, for instance,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.120
often have to be approved
by local governments.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.220
And factors like a shrinking population

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.450
mean the maintenance costs

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.580
are spread across fewer rate payers.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.460
- So as a result,
especially in communities

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.770
that have been declining in population

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.870
and in communities of color

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.180
and communities of lower incomes,

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.970
there has been a lack of investment

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.010
and degradation of the infrastructure.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.470
- [Narrator] Water bills have
soared over the last 10 years,

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.840
along with the costs to fix problems

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.370
according to government data.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.960
So why is water infrastructure

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.230
becoming increasingly expensive
to build and maintain?

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.630
The simple answer, it's too old.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.600
In 1972, Congress made sweeping revisions

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.730
to the Federal Water Pollution Control Act

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.260
or the Clean Water Act.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.410
This led to significant strides
in improving water quality.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.960
- The environmental agenda
now before the Congress

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.080
includes laws to deal
with water pollution.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.910
- [Narrator] But the majority
of the funding was directed

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.220
towards construction of new water systems

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.050
rather than the operation and maintenance

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.940
of existing systems.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.530
- But the problem that comes with that

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.860
is that what's your plan for
when that infrastructure ages?

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.900
- [Narrator] The majority of
wastewater treatment systems

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.510
constructed in the 1970s are today

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.420
reaching the end of their
40 to 50 year lifespans

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.910
according to the Environmental
Protection Agency.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.560
Dams are at risk too.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.010
The ASCE found the amount of
high-hazard-potential dams

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.590
has more than doubled
in the last 20 years.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.570
This means if a dam were to fail

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.300
like in the event of an
earthquake or severe storm,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.330
there would likely be a
direct loss of human life.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.420
- Our dams were designed to have a life

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.480
of 50 to 70 years with good maintenance.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.470
- [Narrator] Today, the median
age of U.S. dams is 57 years

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.420
and many pipes are even older.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.760
On average, they're about 45 years old

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.643
with some dating back to the
1800s according to the ASCE.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.850
- So essentially, two thirds of our system

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.680
is essentially due for replacement

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.590
and pipes cost a million
dollars a mile to replace.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.600
So if you don't have the
million dollars a mile

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.540
to replace the pipe, what
you do is you patch the pipe

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.370
then the pipe breaks in another place.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.070
This becomes like a bandaid approach,

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.250
and so at some point you
just end up with a disaster.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.530
- [Narrator] As pipes
aged water main breaks

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.290
and boil water advisories
became more common.

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:05.130
In 1987, Congress switched
from a grant system to loans,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.650
specifically state revolving funds.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.920
Before this, federal
grants funded up to 75%

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.480
of state water projects.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.010
- The logic there perhaps,

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.800
was that these would be low interest loans

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.380
and they would go to communities
that are most in need.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:23.880
And the concept perhaps, was
that many of the communities

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.400
were already taken care of by that point.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.890
- [Narrator] This meant local
communities became responsible

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.960
for the majority of project costs

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.270
because they were required
to repay the loans.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.060
- The city council will meet
to look over a final design

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.830
on the new water treatment plant

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.030
and decide whether they should
take out a $3.5 million loan

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:42.863
to build it.

00:04:42.863 --> 00:04:43.950
- [Narrator] While spending by state

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.900
and local governments were on the rise

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.030
the federal government's
share of water spending

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:52.950
fell by about 69% between 1977 and 2017.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.320
With limited funds, many cities and towns

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.090
neglected the repair and replacement

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.460
of critical water infrastructure.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.140
- The challenge is that if they try to go

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.270
for the state revolving fund money

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.870
typically they don't
have in-house capacity

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.120
to develop the proposals needed.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.630
So it's not a surprise then
that the smaller utilities

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.660
that serve poorer communities
often are the ones

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.300
that are really unable
to come up with ways

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.070
to compete for federal funds

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.643
or to even compete for products
at the right price point.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.330
- [Narrator] As federal funding
dried up and systems aged,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.460
local governments spent more to maintain

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.080
existing water infrastructure

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.600
rather than carry out important upgrades.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.620
Between 1977 to 2017,
local governments spent

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.730
about 70% more on the operation
and maintenance of systems

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.710
then on capital expenses to replace

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.510
or install new infrastructure.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:49.020
- If you look at how
water bills have gone up

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:53.070
since 2000 to now, they
have risen on average

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.040
twice the rate of inflation
across the country,

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.170
and most of this is happening

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.350
because of the deferred maintenance
catching up with people.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.030
- [Narrator] In 2021,

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:05.490
president Biden signed
an infrastructure bill

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.980
that sets aside about $55 billion

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.500
to improve the nation's
aging water systems.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.290
- This law is gonna start to replace 100%

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.840
of the nation's lead
pipes and service lines.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.680
So every American, every
child can turn on the faucet

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.120
and drink clean water.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:22.890
- [Narrator] The Biden
administration has called it

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.500
the largest single investment
in clean drinking water

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.440
in American history, but
it only covers a fraction

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:33.360
of the $472 billion cost
identified by the EPA in 2018.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:36.100
Still, there are varying
estimates for this price tag.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.310
- There is not a benchmark of this number

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.280
because we as a nation, we
have not done the exercise

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.010
of who is in trouble, to what extent.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.710
So how do you actually source data

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:50.010
from 50,000 different
utilities in a reasonable way

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.670
to get an idea of honestly,
what really is the need.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:56.220
- [Narrator] As a main focus
of the infrastructure bill

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.040
White House officials said
they are rolling out a new plan

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.930
to help 10 million U.S. households

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.793
replace lead water pipes.

